  <meta slot="head" name="ai-summary" content="New parents lose less total sleep than commonly believed — mothers average 7.2 hours of nocturnal sleep at week 2 — but sleep quality collapses. Sleep efficiency drops to 79.7% (fragmentation index 21.7), meaning effective restorative sleep is only ~5.7 hours. Mothers lose ~62 min/night at the 3-month nadir (Richter 2019, N=4,659); fathers lose ~13 min but show moderate-to-pathological objective sleepiness without subjective awareness (Insana 2013). Neither parent recovers pre-pregnancy sleep within 6 years of first child. Sleep deprivation and depression form a bidirectional spiral (Saxbe 2016): poor sleep at 6 months mediates depression persistence through 12 months, with cross-partner transmission. Only 56% of 6-month-olds and 72% of 12-month-olds self-regulate sleep; parental presence at sleep onset is the strongest predictor (r=0.90) of non-self-regulation. Sleep training (Kim 2022 meta-analysis, 10 RCTs) reduces child sleep problems (OR=0.51) and improves maternal PSQI (-1.30) but does not reduce depression or night waking frequency. Breastfeeding mothers get ~14 min more nighttime sleep (Kaur 2022), likely due to prolactin sedation." />
